Ladybird narrows absolute lengths to single precision, then truncates
them to its 1/64px layout precision. Two 66.666666px flex items
fit in a 133.333333px container, while two exact 66.671875px items wrap.
The conversion applies to both retained length values and inline caches.

Blink’s MinimumValueForLength constructs a LayoutUnit from a fixed
length stored as a float. Its FixedPoint constructors truncate
to 1/64px. WebKit’s Style::Evaluation for Length constructs its
result from the resolved length, and LayoutUnit(double) truncates
to the same grid. Gecko converts CSSPixelLength through Au::from_f32_px,
rounding to 1/60px instead. That grid represents the two-item reduction
without an excess, so all three keep the items together.

Chrome confirms the row, column, calculation, percentage, and overflow
controls. Narrowing before truncation also retains an 80px length
whose trigonometric calculation has a small floating-point error.
Chrome keeps that width at 80px and truncates the related tangent
case to 119.984375px, as Ladybird does.

The absolute-length conversion also changes fractional geometry
in four layout dumps and four screenshot baselines. The nested
SVG antialiasing differs slightly from Chrome both before and after
the change; this is a consequence of quantizing lengths to the
layout grid, not an improvement in SVG precision.
